The function also doesn't handle special characters as this would significantly complicate the implementation, but could be easily added in future versions. As a security practice, never store raw or unhashed password data - always hash it before storing with a salt to protect your users' sensitive information. Also, consider using PHP's built-in functions for handling hashing and generating unique identifiers for optimal performance and security.

Here is a simple implementation of such a service using Spring Boot, which includes many of the requirements you mentioned:

```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestTemplate;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

@Service
public class PokemonService {
    private static final String EXTERNAL_API = "https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon/{id}/";

    @Autowired
    RestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Autowired
    ObjectMapper objectMapper;

    @Cacheable("pokemons")
    public PokemonWeaknesses getPokemonWeaknesses(String pokemonId) {
        Map<String, String> params = new HashMap<>();
        params.put("id", pokemonId);

        ResponseEntity<String> response =  restTemplate.getForEntity(EXTERNAL_API, String.class, params);

        if (response.getStatusCode().is2xxSuccessful()) {
            try {
                Map jsonMap = objectMapper.readValue(response.getBody(), Map.class);

                PokemonWeaknesses pokemonWeaknesses = new PokemonWeaknesses();
                pokemonWeaknesses.setName((String)jsonMap.get("name"));
                pokemonWeaknesses.setType(((List<Map>)((Map)jsonMap.get("types")).get("type")).stream()
                        .map(t -> (String)t.get("name"))
                        .findFirst().orElse(""));

                return pokemonWeaknesses;
            } catch (JsonProcessingException e) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Error parsing external API response", e);
            }
        } else {
            throw new RuntimeException("Unexpected HTTP status code: " + response.getStatusCodeValue());
        }
    }

    @Async
    public CompletableFuture<PokemonWeaknesses> getPokemonWeaknessesAsync(String pokemonId) {
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(getPokemonWeaknesses(pokemonId));
    }
}
```
In this code, the `getPokemonWeaknesses` method calls an external REST API to fetch Pokemon data. The results are cached to prevent redundant requests. 

The `getPokemonWeaknessesAsync` method does the same thing but asynchronously using Spring's `@Async` annotation and Java's `CompletableFuture` class, which can be used in a non-blocking manner. This is beneficial when you need to fetch multiple Pokemons at once or don't want to wait for all requests to complete before proceeding.

