Below is a simple demonstration of a DisjointPairFinder class that incorporates Union-Find structure, with some basic command line interface options. The actual implementation would need to be more complex for integration with external APIs as per your specifications. Also, thread safety needs to be implemented in this scenario as well. It's also advisable to make the DisjointPairFinder class generic instead of hard-coding integer types. 

```java
import java.util.*;

public class DisjointPairFinder {
    private int[] parents;
    private long[] timestamps;

    public void initialize(int size) {
        this.parents = new int[size];
        this.timestamps = new long[size];
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            parents[i] = i;
            timestamps[i] = System.currentTimeMillis();
        }
    }

    public void addElement(int index, int element) {
        parents[index] = find(element);
    }

    public boolean isDisjoint(int x, int y) {
        return find(x) != find(y);
    }

    private int find(int x) {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        if (parents[x] != x) parents[x] = find(parents[x]);
        timestamps[x] += System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
        return parents[x];
    }

    public long getOperationTime(int index) {
        return timestamps[index];
    }
}
```
