```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class DeserializeObject {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(DeserializeObject.class.getName());

    public Object deserializeByteArrayToObject(byte[] serializedObject) {
        ByteArrayInputStream byteInStream = null;
        ObjectInputStream objectInStream = null;
        try {
            byteInStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(serializedObject);
            objectInStream = new ObjectInputStream(byteInStream) {
                protected java.lang.Class<?> resolveClass(java.io.ObjectStreamClass desc) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
                    if (!desc.getName().equals("com.mycompany.MyVersion1")) {
                        logger.warning("Unknown class in the stream: " + desc.getName());
                        return super.resolveClass(desc);
                    }
                    try {
                        // Try to load from current version of this class
                        return Class.forName("com.mycompany.MyVersion1");
                    } catch (Exception ex) {
                        logger.warning("Fallback to load class from previous version: " + desc.getName());
                        return super.resolveClass(desc);
                    }
                };
            };
            return objectInStream.readObject();
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Error deserializing byte array to object", e);
        } finally {
            if (objectInStream != null) {
                try {
                    objectInStream.close();
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    logger.warning("Failed to close ObjectInputStream: " + e.getMessage());
                }
            } else if (byteInStream != null) {
                try {
                    byteInStream.close();
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    logger.warning("Failed to close ByteArrayInputStream: " + e.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This method uses a custom `ObjectInputStream` that overrides the `resolveClass` method, which is used to resolve classes during deserialization. If the class name does not match our expected class name ("com.mycompany.MyVersion1" in this example), it falls back to load from previous version of the class.

If there are any problems with the deserialization (for example, if an unknown class is encountered), a `RuntimeException` is thrown. The method also ensures that both `ObjectInputStream` and `ByteArrayInputStream` are closed in a finally block, even if exceptions occur during reading or closing the streams.

