Here is a JavaScript solution using recursion for DOM tree traversal:

```javascript
function findElements(root, selector, testFunc = () => true, maxDepth = 100) {
    // Check if root is a valid node
    if (!(root instanceof Node)) {
        throw new Error('Invalid root node');
    }

    let matchingElements = [];

    function traverseTree(node, depth) {
        if (depth > maxDepth || !(node instanceof Element)) {
            return; // End recursion if maximum depth is reached or node isn't an element
        }

        // If the current node matches the selector and passes the test function...
        if (node.matches(selector) && testFunc(node)) {
            matchingElements.push(node);
        }

        for (let child of node.children) {
            traverseTree(child, depth + 1); // Recursion on each children of the current node
        }
    }

    try {
        traverseTree(root, 0);
    } catch(error) {
        console.log('Test function error:', error);
    }

    return matchingElements;
}
```
This `findElements` function uses recursion to traverse the DOM tree and collect all elements that match a given CSS selector and pass a test function (optional). It handles invalid inputs, edge cases like non-element nodes, and errors thrown from the test function. The depth of the search is also limited by the optional parameter `maxDepth` for optimization. 

Note: This solution uses native JavaScript's `matches()` method which depends on the browser to support CSS selectors according to their standard (https://caniuse.com/?search=matchselector).

