```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

// A simple 3D point struct
struct Point {
    float x, y, z;
};

// OctreeNode represents one node within the octree.
class OctreeNode {
public:
    Point center; // Center of this node's volume
    std::vector<Point> points; // Points contained in this node
    std::unique_ptr<OctreeNode> children[8]; // Child nodes

    float size; // Size of the cube defining this node's volume
    int depth; // Depth of this node within the octree (root has depth 0)
    int maxDepth; // Maximum allowed depth for subdivision
    int dataCapacity; // Max number of points a node can hold before it needs to be divided further

    OctreeNode(Point center, float size, int depth, int maxDepth, int dataCapacity): 
        center(center), size(size), depth(depth), maxDepth(maxDepth), dataCapacity(dataCapacity) {}

    void addPoint(const Point& point) {

        // If this node has reached its maximum capacity and it's not yet at the maximum depth, subdivide and add the point to one of the child nodes.
        if (points.size() >= dataCapacity && depth < maxDepth) {
            subdivide();

            for(auto& child : children){
                if(isPointInCube(point, child->center, child->size)){
                    child->addPoint(point);
                    break;
                }
            }
        // If this node's capacity hasn't been reached yet or it's at the maximum depth, add the point to this node.
        } else {
            points.push_back(point);
        }
    }

private:
    void subdivide() {

        // Create child nodes for each octant of the cube.
        float newSize = size / 2;
        children[0] = std::make_unique<OctreeNode>(Point{center.x - newSize, center.y + newSize, center.z + newSize}, newSize, depth + 1, maxDepth, dataCapacity);
        children[1] = std::make_unique<OctreeNode>(Point{center.x + newSize, center.y + newSize, center.z + newSize}, newSize, depth + 1, maxDepth, dataCapacity);
        children[2] = std::make_unique<OctreeNode>(Point{center.x - newSize, center.y - newSize, center.z + newSize}, newSize, depth + 1, maxDepth, dataCapacity);
        children[3] = std::make_unique<OctreeNode>(Point{center.x + newSize, center.y - newSize, center.z + newSize}, newSize, depth + 1, maxDepth, dataCapacity);
        children[4] = std::make_unique<OctreeNode>(Point{center.x - newSize, center.y + newSize, center.z - newSize}, newSize, depth + 1, maxDepth, dataCapacity);
        children[5] = std::make_unique<OctreeNode>(Point{center.x + newSize, center.y + newSize, center.z - newSize}, newSize, depth + 1, maxDepth, dataCapacity);
        children[6] = std::make_unique<OctreeNode>(Point{center.x - newSize, center.y - newSize, center.z - newSize}, newSize, depth + 1, maxDepth, dataCapacity);
        children[7] = std::make_unique<OctreeNode>(Point{center.x + newSize, center.y - newSize, center.z - newSize}, newSize, depth + 1, maxDepth, dataCapacity);
    }

    // Checks if a point is within a cube (used for determining which child node to add a point to).
    bool isPointInCube(const Point& pt, const Point& center, float size) {
        return std::abs(pt.x - center.x) <= size && std::abs(pt.y - center.y) <= size && std::abs(pt.z - center.z) <= size;
    }
};
```
In the above code, we have an OctreeNode class that represents one node in our octree. Each node keeps track of its 3D position (center), a list of points contained within it, and pointers to its children nodes. The addPoint method attempts to add a point to this node or one of its children. If the node's data capacity is reached and the maxDepth hasn't been reached yet, the node will subdivide into 8 smaller nodes (octants).

The code does not include functionality for removing points from the octree, but this could easily be added. In a full implementation, you might also want to add methods for querying the data in the octree (e.g., finding all points within a given volume), or optimizing the octree layout based on its contents (by moving nodes with few points around).

