In JavaScript, we can use the Streams API for handling streams of binary data efficiently. The following is an implementation that fulfills all these requirements:

```js
const { Transform } = require('stream');

class BufferStream extends Transform {
    constructor(options) {
        super(options);

        this.bytes = 0;
        this.bufferData = Buffer.alloc(0);
    }

    _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
        if (!Buffer.isBuffer(chunk)) return callback(new Error('Invalid chunk: Not a buffer'));

        // Append new data to existing buffer
        this.bufferData = Buffer.concat([this.bufferData, chunk]);
        this.bytes += chunk.length;

        callback();
    }

    _flush(callback) {
        this.push(this.bufferData);
        callback();
    }
}

const copyBytesFromStream = (streamSrc, bytesToCopy, callback) => {
    if (!streamSrc || !Number.isFinite(bytesToCopy)) return callback('Invalid parameters');

    // Create a new BufferStream instance to handle data chunks
    const bufferStream = new BufferStream();

    streamSrc.pipe(bufferStream)
        .on('error', err => {
            console.log('Error in the streaming: ', err);
            callback(err);
        })
        .on('data', (chunk) => {
            // Stop reading from the source if we've copied enough bytes
            if (bufferStream.bytes >= bytesToCopy) streamSrc.unpipe();
        });

    bufferStream.once('readable', () => {
        let newBuffer = bufferStream.read(bytesToCopy);

        // Check for an incomplete read and handle the case where we haven't copied enough bytes
        if (newBuffer.length < bytesToCopy) {
            console.log('Data chunk is not complete');

            callback({ message: 'Data chunk is not complete', newBuffer });
        } else {
            callback(null, newBuffer);
        }
    });
};
```

You can use this function in a test file like so:

```js
const fs = require('fs');
const readStream = fs.createReadStream('/path/to/file.txt');

copyBytesFromStream(readStream, 1024, (err, newBuffer) => {
    if (err) return console.error('Error: ', err);

    // Do something with newBuffer...
});
```

This function creates a custom Transform stream that concatenates all incoming data into one large Buffer. The `copyBytesFromStream` function takes an input Stream and a desired number of bytes to copy, then sets up a pipeline from the input Stream through our Transform stream. When enough data is read (determined by comparing total read bytes with the target), it unpipes from the input Stream to prevent further unnecessary reads.

This solution is scalable due to the efficient handling of streaming data via JavaScript's built-in streams API, which supports backpressure and flow control. Also, error handling is robust for potential issues like invalid chunk lengths or network interruptions. The clean separation between stream logic (Transform) and copy function makes it easy to test each part separately.

