Here's a basic implementation of your requirements:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>
#include <errno.h>

int modify_filename(const char *original_filename, char *new_filename) {
    const char* valid_extensions[] = { ".txt", ".jpg", ".png" };
    int len = strlen(original_filename);

    if (len == 0 || len > 255) {
        errno = EINVAL;
        return -1;
    }

    const char *last_dot = strrchr(original_filename, '.');

    if (!last_dot || last_dot == original_filename) {
        errno = ENOENT;
        return -2;
    }

    int digits = 0;

    if (isdigit(original_filename[len-1])) {
        digits = original_filename[len-1] - '0';
    } else {
        digits = 3;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < sizeof(valid_extensions) / sizeof(valid_extensions[0]); ++i) {
        if (strcmp(last_dot, valid_extensions[i]) == 0) {
            snprintf(new_filename, len + digits + 1, "%.*s%d%s", last_dot - original_filename, original_filename, digits, last_dot);
            return 0;
        }
    }

    errno = EFTYPE;
    return -3;
}
```

This function checks the length of the filename and if it's not empty or exceeds a certain size. It also checks if there is an extension by finding the last dot in the original filename and checking if it exists after the first character, since filenames without extensions are invalid according to your requirements. The function then determines how many digits should be appended based on the last character of the filename. If it's a digit, that many digits will be appended; otherwise, three digits will be appended by default. Then, it checks if the extension is among the valid ones and, if so, formats the new filename accordingly.

Please note that this function does not support multiple extensions (e.g., "example.txt.jpg") or filenames without directories. Also, it doesn't validate each character of the filename to ensure it's suitable for use in a file path on your system, but you could easily add such functionality if necessary.

