Here is a basic implementation of a C# class that includes two methods for AES-CBC and AES-GCM decryption, as per your specifications. Please note this example assumes you are using .NET Core 3.0+ which supports AesGcm for GCM mode encryption/decryption.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

public class Decryptor {
    private static readonly RNGCryptoServiceProvider rng = new RNGCryptoServiceProvider();

    public byte[] DecryptAesCbc(byte[] ciphertext, byte[] key, byte[] iv) {
        using (var aesAlg = Aes.Create()) {
            if (aesAlg == null) throw new Exception("Failed to create AES algorithm instance");

            aesAlg.Mode = CipherMode.CBC;
            aesAlg.KeySize = key.Length * 8;
            aesAlg.BlockSize = 128; // for CBC mode
            aesAlg.Padding = PaddingMode.PKCS7; // padding scheme

            aesAlg.Key = key;
            aesAlg.IV = iv;

            var decryptor = aesAlg.CreateDecryptor(aesAlg.Key, aesAlg.IV);

            return decryptor.TransformFinalBlock(ciphertext, 0, ciphertext.Length);
        }
    }

    public byte[] DecryptAesGcm(byte[] ciphertext, byte[] key) {
        using (var aesGcm = new AesGcm(key)) {
            var nonceSizeInBytes = AesGcm.NonceByteSizes.MaxSize;
            var tagSizeInBytes = 128 / 8; // for GCM mode

            Span<byte> nonce = ciphertext.AsSpan(0, nonceSizeInBytes);
            Span<byte> cipher = ciphertext.AsSpan(nonceSizeInBytes, ciphertext.Length - nonceSizeInBytes - tagSizeInBytes);
            Span<byte> tag = ciphertext.AsSpan(ciphertext.Length - tagSizeInBytes, tagSizeInBytes);

            Span<byte> plaintext = new byte[cipher.Length]; // allocate a new array for the plaintext 
            aesGcm.Decrypt(nonce, cipher, tag, plaintext);

            return plaintext.ToArray();
        }
    }

    public static void Main() {
        var decryptor = new Decryptor();

        // Generate a random key and iv for testing purposes
        byte[] key = new byte[32];
        rng.GetBytes(key);
        byte[] iv = new byte[16];
        rng.GetBytes(iv);

        var ciphertextCbcMode = decryptor.DecryptAesCbc(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("ciphertext"), key, iv);
        Console.WriteLine(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(ciphertextCbcMode));

        // You need to have a valid AES-GCM ciphertext for testing this method
        var ciphertextGcmMode = decryptor.DecryptAesGcm(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("ciphertext"), key);
        Console.WriteLine(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(ciphertextGcmMode));
    }
}
```
Please be aware that AES-CBC and AES-GCM are different encryption modes with their own security properties, so using them interchangeably can lead to serious vulnerabilities if misused. Always choose the right mode for your use case. Also note that you need to provide a valid AES-GCM ciphertext to test the `DecryptAesGcm` method.

Here is an example implementation of such a method using Java Serialization. The code uses a `ConfigurationManager` to handle the configuration file. We also use a cache manager to store frequently accessed class names and configurations in memory, and we log every event with a logger. 

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;
import org.apache.log4j.Logger;

public class ObjectDeserializer {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(ObjectDeserializer.class);

    // A map to cache frequently accessed class names and configurations
    private static Map<String, Class> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public static <T> T deserialize(byte[] data) {
        ConfigurationManager configManager = ConfigurationManager.getInstance();
        String className = configManager.getClassNameFromConfig();

        Class clazz;
        if (cache.containsKey(className)) {
            clazz = cache.get(className);
        } else {
            try {
                clazz = Class.forName(className);
                cache.put(className, clazz);
            } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
                logger.error("The class of the object being deserialized is not found", e);
                throw new RuntimeException("Failed to find the class during deserialization", e);
            }
        }

        try(ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
            ObjectInput in = new ObjectInputStream(bis)) {

            return clazz.cast(in.readObject());

        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to deserialize the object", e);
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to deserialize the byte array into an object", e);
        } 
    }
}
```

This class can be used like this:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Assuming 'data' is a byte array obtained from serialization.
    byte[] data;
    Object obj = ObjectDeserializer.deserialize(data);
}
```
This class has limitations in terms of performance and scalability, specifically when dealing with concurrent deserialization requests with large byte arrays, because reading from the same stream is not thread-safe due to how `readObject()` operates. To address this problem, you may need to implement a custom serialized format that supports random access reads, or switch to a different data format (e.g., Protocol Buffers) that supports faster deserialization with less memory overhead.

