Here is an abstract representation of how you could structure your C function named `initSecurityProviders` that dynamically discovers and initializes a set of available cryptographic hash functions and block ciphers from an external library. This solution assumes that the external library provides an API for listing available providers and an initialization routine.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Struct representing a provider
typedef struct securityProvider {
    const char* name;
    void (*implementation)(void);  // Function pointer to the provider's implementation
    void* params;                  // Pointer to any necessary parameters for the provider
    struct securityProvider* next; // Pointer to the next provider in the linked list
} securityProvider;

// Prototype of the external library function to get providers
void* (*getProviders)();

securityProvider* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void initSecurityProviders() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); // Ensure thread safety

    if (head != NULL) {  // If providers are already initialized, return
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        return;
    }

    // Call the external library's API to get available providers
    void* providerNames = getProviders();

    if (providerNames == NULL) {  // Handle error in case of invalid or missing providers
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to retrieve security providers.\n");
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        return;
    }

    char** provider = (char**)providerNames;

    // Iterate through the list of provider names and initialize each one
    for (; *provider != NULL; ++provider) {
        securityProvider* newProvider = malloc(sizeof(securityProvider));  // Allocate memory for a new provider

        if (newProvider == NULL) {  // Handle error in case of memory allocation failure
            fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory for a new provider.\n");
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
            return;
        }

        newProvider->name = *provider;
        // Here you would typically call the initialization function of each provider from the external library
        // For now, we will just set a placeholder implementation function pointer
        newProvider->implementation = NULL;  
        newProvider->params = NULL; 

        if (head == NULL) {  // If this is the first provider, update head of linked list
            newProvider->next = NULL;
            head = newProvider;
        } else {  // Otherwise, append to end of existing list
            securityProvider* current = head;

            while (current->next != NULL) {
                current = current->next;
            }

            current->next = newProvider;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);  // Release lock
}
```
This function first locks a mutex to ensure thread safety. If providers are already initialized, it returns without doing anything. Otherwise, it calls the external library's API to get the list of available provider names. For each name, it allocates memory for a new `securityProvider` struct, fills in its fields, and appends it to the linked list of providers. It also implements error handling for both memory allocation failures and invalid or missing provider names. Finally, it unlocks the mutex before returning.

