Here's a basic implementation that should meet your requirements. This example demonstrates fetching the latest commit message from a specific branch by executing a Git command and capturing both standard output and error streams using `ProcessBuilder`.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class GitCommitFetcher {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length < 3) {
            System.out.println("Usage: GitCommitFetcher <repositoryPath> <branchName> <outputFilePath>");
            return;
        }

        String repositoryPath = args[0];
        String branchName = args[1];
        String outputFilePath = args[2];

        // Delete any existing output file
        File outputFile = new File(outputFilePath);
        if (outputFile.exists()) {
            try {
                Files.delete(outputFile.toPath());
            } catch (IOException e) {
                System.out.println("Failed to delete the output file: " + outputFilePath);
                return;
            }
        }

        // Prepare Git command
        ProcessBuilder processBuilder = new ProcessBuilder()
                .directory(new File(repositoryPath))
                .command("/usr/bin/git", "log", branchName, "-1", "--pretty=%B");

        try {
            long startTime = System.nanoTime();

            // Run Git command and capture output
            Process process = processBuilder.start();
            boolean finishedInTime = process.waitFor(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

            if (!finishedInTime) {
                System.out.println("Git command timed out");
                return;
            }

            long endTime = System.nanoTime();

            // Handle Git command output and error streams
            String gitOutput = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()))
                    .lines()
                    .collect(Collectors.joining("\n"));

            if (gitOutput.isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("No commit message found for branch " + branchName);
                return;
            }

            // Write Git command output to the output file
            Files.writeString(outputFile.toPath(), gitOutput, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);

            int exitValue = process.exitValue();
            System.out.println("Git command execution details: Start Time - " + startTime 
                    + ", End Time - " + endTime + ", Exit Value - " + exitValue);
        } catch (IOException | InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("Failed to execute the Git command: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```
This code assumes that `/usr/bin/git` is the path of the Git executable in your system. The timeout duration is 5 seconds and can be adjusted as necessary based on your requirements. Error handling has been kept minimal for brevity, but you would ideally want to handle different types of exceptions separately and provide more specific feedback to the user where possible.

