Here is a basic implementation in C using Pthreads. This code does not support deallocations as part of this task. It allocates one record at a time for simplicity. In real scenario, we'd have to maintain a linked list or an array that tracks all the allocated records and can be used to delete them when needed.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct record {
    time_t timestamp;
    int eventID;
    char* message;
} Record;

Record *records = NULL;
size_t recordsSize = 0, recordsCapacity = 0;
pthread_mutex_t mutex;

void initRecords(int num) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);  // Lock to ensure exclusive access.

    if (records == NULL) {
        records = malloc(num * sizeof(Record));
        if (records == NULL) {
            perror("Error allocating memory for records");
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);  // Unlock before returning.
            return;
        }

        recordsCapacity = num;
    } else if (recordsSize + num > recordsCapacity) {
        recordsCapacity *= 2;  // Double the capacity each time we run out of space.
        records = realloc(records, recordsCapacity * sizeof(Record));

        if (records == NULL) {
            perror("Error allocating memory for records");
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);  // Unlock before returning.
            return;
        }
    }

    for (int i = recordsSize; i < recordsSize + num; ++i) {
        time_t t = rand() % 1000000000 + 1000000000; // Timestamp range is [10^9, 2*10^9).
        int eid = rand() % 1000; // Event ID range is [0, 1000).

        records[i].timestamp = t;
        records[i].eventID = eid;

        // For simplicity, let's assume the message can be of length at most 256.
        records[i].message = malloc(256); 
        if (records[i].message == NULL) {
            perror("Error allocating memory for message");
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);  // Unlock before returning.
            return;
        }

        sprintf(records[i].message, "Message #%d", i); // Or set a dynamic message here.
    }

    recordsSize += num;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);  // Unlock after operation is finished.
}
```
This function allocates memory for a number of `record` structures and initializes them with random timestamps, event IDs and messages. If the system runs out of allocated space during initialization, it doubles the capacity to ensure efficient use of memory. For message content, this implementation assumes that messages can be of length at most 256 characters long, which can be adjusted as per requirement. The code also handles errors during memory allocation by checking for NULL pointers and printing error messages using `perror`. Lastly, it is thread safe thanks to a mutex locking mechanism.

