For the sake of brevity, I'll provide an example of how to build a middleware that handles climate data, rate limits and API quotas using ASP .NET Core Middleware:

```csharp
public class RealTimeClimateMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    private readonly int maxRequestsPerMinute; // define your threshold here
    private static SemaphoreSlim semaphore = new SemaphoreSlim(1, 1);
    private DateTime firstRequestTimeStamp = DateTime.UtcNow;
    private int requestCount = 0;

    public RealTimeClimateMiddleware(RequestDelegate next)
    {
        _next = next;
        // Initialize maxRequestsPerMinute with your desired value
    }

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext httpContext, IClimateService climateService)
    {
        if (await semaphore.WaitAsync(0))
        {
            try
            {
                var now = DateTime.UtcNow;

                // Check if more than one minute has passed since first request
                if ((now - firstRequestTimeStamp).TotalMinutes >= 1)
                {
                    firstRequestTimeStamp = now;
                    requestCount = 0;
                }

                // If we've reached our limit, return 429 (Too Many Requests) and don't forward the request to the next middleware
                if (requestCount >= maxRequestsPerMinute)
                {
                    httpContext.Response.StatusCode = StatusCodes.Status429TooManyRequests;
                    return;
                }

                // Otherwise, increment our counter and continue processing the request
                requestCount++;
                await _next(httpContext);
            }
            finally
            {
                semaphore.Release();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, I've used SemaphoreSlim to ensure that all requests are processed one at a time and prevent race conditions. This is not the only way of implementing rate limiting though. Depending on your requirements you might want to use a more advanced solution like Token Bucket or Leaky Bucket algorithms.

Remember that the code above doesn't handle caching, it's just an example of how you can implement middleware for rate limiting in ASP .NET Core.

