Here is an example of how you might implement this in C# using LINQ to XML with XPath queries and asynchronous programming with async/await constructs:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Xml.Linq;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Runtime.Caching;

public class XMLParser
{
    private static readonly MemoryCache Cache = new MemoryCache("XMLCache");

    // Method to fetch and parse the XML document from external API
    public async Task<XDocument> FetchAndParse(string url)
    {
        // Check if URL is validated
        if (String.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(url)) throw new ArgumentException("URL cannot be null or empty");

        XDocument xmlDoc;

        if (Cache[url] != null) 
        {
            // If XML data exists in cache, return it directly
            xmlDoc = (XDocument) Cache.Get(url);
        } 
        else 
        {
            using (HttpClient client = new HttpClient())
            {
                string responseBody = await client.GetStringAsync(url).ConfigureAwait(false);

                // Parse the XML document and cache it for later use
                xmlDoc = XDocument.Parse(responseBody);
                CacheItemPolicy policy = new CacheItemPolicy();
                policy.AbsoluteExpiration = DateTimeOffset.Now.AddHours(1);
                Cache.Set(url, xmlDoc, policy);
            }
        }

        return xmlDoc;
    }

    // Method to get the first matching element in XML using XPath
    public XElement GetFirstMatchingElement(XDocument doc, string name)
    {
        if (doc == null || String.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(name)) throw new ArgumentException("Invalid document or name");

        var elements = doc.Descendants().Where(e => e.Name.LocalName == name);
        int count = elements.Count();

        if (count == 0) throw new Exception("No matching element found");

        if (count > 1) throw new Exception("Multiple matching elements found");

        return elements.FirstOrDefault();  // Returns the first matching element
    }
}
```

The `XMLParser` class has two methods:
- The method FetchAndParse, which takes a URL as input and fetches an XML document from an external API using HttpClient. It also caches the result for future use to improve performance. If no data exists in cache for the provided URL, it makes a GET request to fetch the data and parses it into an XDocument object. The parsed XDocument is then stored in memory cache for later use.
- The method GetFirstMatchingElement takes an XDocument and a name as input and retrieves the first matching element using LINQ to XML with XPath. It throws a custom exception if no or multiple elements are found with the given name.

Please note that this is a simplified version of handling XML parsing and caching. Depending on your use case, you might need to handle additional edge cases like network timeouts, exceptions during document parse etc. Furthermore, always be careful when using `XDocument.Parse` as it can throw an OutOfMemoryException for very large documents that don't fit into memory. If this is a possibility in your project, consider streaming or other strategies to avoid loading the entire XML document into memory at once.

