Here is a simplified example of how you can use JJWT library to generate and validate JSON Web Tokens:

```java
import io.jsonwebtoken.*;
import java.util.Date;

public class JwtExample {
    public String generateJWT(String id, String issuer, String subject, long ttlMillis) {
        SignatureAlgorithm signatureAlgorithm = SignatureAlgorithm.HS256; // Use your KMS private key here

        long nowMillis = System.currentTimeMillis();
        Date now = new Date(nowMillis);

        JwtBuilder builder = Jwts.builder()
                .setId(id)
                .setIssuedAt(now)
                .setSubject(subject)
                .setIssuer(issuer)
                .signWith(signatureAlgorithm, "your-kms-private-key"); // Use your KMS private key here

        if (ttlMillis >= 0) {
            long expMillis = nowMillis + ttlMillis;
            Date exp = new Date(expMillis);
            builder.setExpiration(exp);
        }

        return builder.compact();
    }

    public boolean validateJWT(String jwt) {
        try {
            Jwts.parser().setSigningKey("your-kms-public-key").parseClaimsJws(jwt); // Use your KMS public key here
            return true;
        } catch (SignatureException e) {
            // Invalid signature
            System.out.println("Invalid JWT Signature");
        } catch (MalformedJwtException e) {
            // Invalid token
            System.out.println("Malformed JWT Token");
        } catch (ExpiredJwtException e) {
            // Expired token
            System.out.println("Expired JWT Token");
        } catch (UnsupportedJwtException e) {
            // Unsupported token
            System.out.println("Unsupported JWT Token");
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            // claims string is empty
            System.out.println("Empty Claims String");
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```
This is a simplified example and does not include the caching, versioning switching between KMS providers, handling 1000 concurrent requests efficiently, implementing token refresh mechanism etc. It just shows how to generate and validate JWT using a private key stored in a secure Key Management Service (KMS).

