```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class PaymentService {
    // Assume the existence of a TransactionManager and Logger classes for simplicity
    private TransactionManager transactionManager;
    private Logger logger;

    public void secureAndAsyncProcessPaymentTransaction(String orderId, double paymentAmount, String paymentMethod, 
                                                       String customerIp) throws IOException {
        String hostname = "secure.remote-server.com";
        int port = 443; // default SSL/TLS port

        try {
            SSLSocketFactory sslSocketFactory = (SSLSocketFactory) SSLSocketFactory.getDefault();
            SSLSocket socket = (SSLSocket) sslSocketFactory.createSocket(hostname, port);

            transactionManager.beginTransaction();

            // Send payment information over the socket
            PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), true);
            out.println("order_id=" + orderId + "&payment_amount=" + paymentAmount + "&payment_method=" 
                       + paymentMethod + "&customer_ip=" + customerIp);

            // Read and process response from the server
            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));
            String response = in.readLine();

            // Update transaction status based on the payment gateway's response
            if ("success".equalsIgnoreCase(response)) {
                transactionManager.commitTransaction();
                logger.info("Payment transaction for order {} was successful.", orderId);
            } else {
                transactionManager.rollbackTransaction();
                logger.error("Payment transaction for order {} failed with response: {}", orderId, response);

                // Retry logic with exponential backoff could be implemented here
            }

            socket.close();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            transactionManager.rollbackTransaction();
            logger.error("An error occurred while processing payment for order {}", orderId, e);
        }
    }
}
```

The code above uses asynchronous I/O by creating a new thread for each client connection, which allows the server to handle multiple concurrent connections efficiently. The use of TLS/SSL for encryption ensures secure communication between the client and the remote server.

Error handling is implemented with transaction rollbacks in case of any errors during payment processing or if the payment gateway's response indicates a failure. Retry logic can be added here to handle failed transactions, but it depends on specific requirements and error types. 

