Here is a C++ implementation of `safeFileAppend` according to your specifications:

```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>

int safeFileAppend(const char* filePath, const char* buffer) {
    // Check if the file path is not null and does not exceed a maximum length.
    if (filePath == nullptr || strlen(filePath) > 4096) {
        std::cerr << "Invalid file path!" << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    // Open the file in append mode.
    std::ofstream outputFile;
    outputFile.open(filePath, std::ios_base::app);

    if (!outputFile) {
        std::cerr << "Failed to open the file!" << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    // Append the data from the buffer to the file.
    outputFile << buffer;

    if (!outputFile) {
        std::cerr << "Failed to append data to the file!" << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    int bytesWritten = strlen(buffer);

    // Close the file.
    outputFile.close();

    if (outputFile.fail()) {
        std::cerr << "Failed to close the file!" << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    return bytesWritten;
}
```
This function handles resource management using RAII, specifically `std::ofstream` automatically closes when it is destroyed. The function validates and performs error checks at every step of the process to ensure that it behaves as expected. It logs errors to the standard error output (cerr). If an error occurs, it returns -1; otherwise, it returns the number of bytes successfully appended to the file.

