Here is an implementation of such a function in C# using locking, exception handling, and caching. However, please note that .NET does not support multithreaded I/O operations well (in contrast to many other languages), so the solution can be improved by making better use of asynchronous programming.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;
using System.Threading;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;

public class CustomDataStructure {} // Your custom data structure goes here

public static class BinaryFileLoader 
{
    private static readonly ReaderWriterLockSlim cacheLock = new ReaderWriterLockSlim();
    private static ConcurrentDictionary<string, CustomDataStructure> cache = 
        new ConcurrentDictionary<string, CustomDataStructure>();

    public static CustomDataStructure LoadFromFile(string filePath) 
    {
        if (cache.TryGetValue(filePath, out var data)) 
            return data;

        try 
        {
            cacheLock.EnterUpgradeableReadLock(); // Enter upgradeable read lock to support concurrent reading and writing

            // If not in cache yet, enter the write lock to update the cache
            if (!cache.TryGetValue(filePath, out data)) 
            {
                cacheLock.EnterWriteLock();

                try 
                {
                    // Check again if another thread already updated the cache while we were waiting for the write lock
                    if (cache.ContainsKey(filePath)) 
                        return cache[filePath];

                    data = LoadFromFileInternal(filePath);
                    cache[filePath] = data;
                } 
                finally 
                {
                    cacheLock.ExitWriteLock(); // Always make sure to release the lock
                }
            }

            return data;
        } 
        finally 
        {
            cacheLock.ExitUpgradeableReadLock();
        }
    }

    private static CustomDataStructure LoadFromFileInternal(string filePath) 
    {
        try 
        {
            using (var stream = File.OpenRead(filePath)) 
            {
                var formatter = new BinaryFormatter();

                // Logging the loading process here could be helpful for debugging
                Console.WriteLine("Loading file: " + filePath);

                return (CustomDataStructure)formatter.Deserialize(stream);
            }
        } 
        catch (Exception ex) 
        {
            // Log the error here with additional details that could be helpful for debugging
            Console.WriteLine("Error loading file: " + filePath + "\n" + ex.Message);

            throw;
        }
    }
}
```
This function uses a reader/writer lock to allow multiple concurrent reads, but when writing (when the data is not in cache), it enters a write lock. This ensures that no other thread can read from or write to the cache simultaneously, thus ensuring thread safety while still allowing efficient concurrency for reads. 

Please note that this solution assumes that your CustomDataStructure can be serialized and deserialized using BinaryFormatter. If it is not, you'll need a different way to load data from file or serialize/deserialize the data structure.

