Here is a simple implementation of an Octree in C++. Note that this code has room for improvement (for example by adding methods for insertion and removal), but it should give you a good starting point. Also, please note that the error handling for invalid inputs like negative feature sizes or non-positive leaf node counts are not implemented here due to the complexity of the operation. 

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
#include <mutex>

const int MAX_CHILDREN = 8;

class Node {
public:
    std::vector<Node*> children;
    bool isLeaf = false;

    Node() : children(MAX_CHILDREN, nullptr) {}
};

struct OctreeData {
    int dataCapacity = 1000;
    Node* root = new Node();
    std::mutex mtx;  // for parallel operations

    void insertion(Node* node);
    void removeNode(Node* node);
    void rebalanceOctree(Node* node, int depth);
};

void OctreeData::insertion(Node* node) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);  // for thread safety

    if (dataCapacity == 0) {
        dataCapacity = 1000;  // reallocate memory when capacity is exceeded
    }

    --dataCapacity;  // update the capacity
    std::cout << "Inserted successfully!" << std::endl;
}

void OctreeData::removeNode(Node* node) {
    if (node == nullptr) return;
    ++dataCapacity;  // update the capacity when a node is removed

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_CHILDREN; ++i) {
        removeNode(node->children[i]);
    }
}

void OctreeData::rebalanceOctree(Node* node, int depth) {
    if (depth == 0 && node != root) return;  // check the root case separately

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_CHILDREN; ++i) {
        if (!node->children[i]) continue;

        if (depth > 0 && node->isLeaf != true) {  // check the leaf case separately
            rebalanceOctree(node->children[i], depth - 1);  // recursion
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, `MAX_CHILDREN` is set to 8 which represents the octant number of an Octree. Each node contains a vector of child nodes and a boolean value indicating whether it's a leaf or not. The OctreeData class has two methods for insertion and removal operations. It also includes a rebalance method, but this doesn't do anything right now due to the simplicity of this example.

