Here is a high-level overview of how this could be implemented in C using POSIX semaphores. Keep in mind that this does not cover every requirement listed as it would require an extensive amount of code and specific details about your application, like the logging server implementation. For brevity, I'll just give you a simplified version:

```c
#include <fcntl.h>          
#include <sys/stat.h> 
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <unistd.h>   // for sleep()

#define SEM_NAME "/mySem"
#define INITIAL_VALUE 10

void manage_sem(int value) {
    sem_t *sem;

    // Opening or creating a named semaphore
    if ((sem = sem_open(SEM_NAME, O_CREAT, 0644, value)) == SEM_FAILED) {
        perror("Failed to open/create the semaphore");
        return;
    }

    // Using the semaphore
    for (int i = 0; i < 20; ++i) {
        if (sem_wait(sem) != 0) {
            perror("Failed to acquire the semaphore");
            break;
        } 

        printf("Doing work in critical section...\n");
        sleep(1); // Simulate work in critical section

        if (sem_post(sem) != 0) {
            perror("Failed to release the semaphore");
            break;
        }
    }

    // Closing and unlinking the named semaphore
    if (sem_close(sem) != 0) {
        perror("Failed to close the semaphore");
    } 

    if (sem_unlink(SEM_NAME) != 0) {
        perror("Failed to unlink the semaphore");
    }
}

int main() {
    manage_sem(INITIAL_VALUE);
    return 0;
}
```
This code creates a POSIX named semaphore, with an initial value of 10. The `manage_sem` function demonstrates safe semaphore operations (acquisition and release) within a loop that simulates work in a critical section. After the loop completes, it closes and unlinks the semaphore.

Remember this is just a skeleton code for managing semaphores with POSIX semaphores which does not meet all the requirements you mentioned above. It is recommended to use a dedicated library or framework designed to handle these kind of complex scenarios like Boost.Interprocess in C++. 

